1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,360
On one side, pounding surf. On the other, looming mountains and rugged gorges.

2
00:00:09,361 --> 00:00:11,520
Building it pushed

3
00:00:11,521 --> 00:00:14,152
road and bridge engineers to new heights.

4
00:00:14,153 --> 00:00:17,040
Now, buckle up and enjoy the ride on the most

5
00:00:17,041 --> 00:00:21,600
spectacular roadway in America, the Pacific Coast Highway.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,440
It took decades for construction crews to carve out, build up, bridge over, tunnel through,

7
00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:48,700
and blast their way up all of America's west coast.

8
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:56,440
The Pacific Coast Highway has become the generally accepted name of the nearly 1,700-mile-long

9
00:00:56,441 --> 00:00:59,400
highway system that runs from Mexico to Canada.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
The overall route is made up of three designated roads.

11
00:01:05,001 --> 00:01:09,000
The southernmost 79 miles is now the Five Freeway,

12
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:13,000
a multi-lane thoroughfare to accommodate all types of traffic,

13
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:18,000
including commerce, military, and commuters.

14
00:01:18,001 --> 00:01:21,000
Just below California's San Juan Capistrano,

15
00:01:21,001 --> 00:01:26,000
Highway 1 begins its 639-mile scenic route.

16
00:01:26,001 --> 00:01:29,000
It moves through exclusive beachfront communities,

17
00:01:29,001 --> 00:01:33,000
including the movie star colony of Malibu.

18
00:01:33,001 --> 00:01:36,000
Built to celebrate some of the most beautiful coastline in the world,

19
00:01:36,001 --> 00:01:42,000
the Pacific Coast Highway is an integral part of the Southern California lifestyle.

20
00:01:42,001 --> 00:01:47,000
In Central California, Route 1 takes on a whole new personality

21
00:01:47,001 --> 00:01:51,000
as it clings to the very edge of the coastal mountain range

22
00:01:51,001 --> 00:01:55,000
and passes over some uniquely devised bridges.

23
00:01:55,001 --> 00:01:59,260
The bridges were all designed by engineers from the Division of Highways,

24
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:04,460
and they constitute some of the most spectacular bridges that we have in the state today.

25
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:11,620
Halfway up California, the road crosses over one of the greatest engineering feats of all time,

26
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:15,640
San Francisco's Golden Gate Bridge.

27
00:02:18,540 --> 00:02:23,240
In the northern half of the state, Route 1 hugs the thinly populated shoreline.

28
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:32,000
It's in this area that California Route 1 gives up and lets Highway 101 take over.

29
00:02:32,001 --> 00:02:39,440
The 101 serves nearly all of Oregon's coastline and features an impressive set of brilliantly

30
00:02:39,441 --> 00:02:41,000
engineered bridges.

31
00:02:41,001 --> 00:02:47,060
Then it continues on through the wilds of Washington to the very northwest corner of

32
00:02:47,061 --> 00:02:49,560
the continental United States.

33
00:02:49,561 --> 00:02:55,500
While the spectacular vistas and unique wildlife attract many tourists all along the Pacific Coast Highway,

34
00:02:56,920 --> 00:03:02,280
it is one of those rare cases where a road has become a destination in itself.

35
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:08,120
It's beautiful, but it's also a wonderful road as roads go.

36
00:03:09,020 --> 00:03:15,100
It twirls, it climbs, it drops, and if you have a responsive sports car or a motorcycle,

37
00:03:15,101 --> 00:03:19,400
Well, Pacific Coast Highway is the way you want to go because it's just great fun.

38
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:25,700
At the same time, it can be a little bit challenging, a little intimidating, because it is risky.

39
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:29,500
It's not a terribly safe road as highways go.

40
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,980
It was over 100 years ago when surveyors first started planning California's highway system.

41
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:42,120
But their initial goal was simply to link county seats to inland highways.

42
00:03:42,121 --> 00:03:44,920
It didn't make sense to put a road along the coast,

43
00:03:44,921 --> 00:03:47,160
except for San Francisco and San Diego.

44
00:03:47,161 --> 00:03:48,660
Nobody lived along the coast.

45
00:03:48,661 --> 00:03:53,400
It means, by definition, you're going to be going by cliffs.

46
00:03:53,401 --> 00:03:57,160
You're going to be in areas that are subjected to storms,

47
00:03:57,161 --> 00:03:59,400
where there's going to be flooding,

48
00:03:59,401 --> 00:04:02,440
where there's going to be a very corrosive environment

49
00:04:02,441 --> 00:04:03,800
of the salt water.

50
00:04:03,801 --> 00:04:06,040
From a practical standpoint,

51
00:04:06,041 --> 00:04:09,080
it's very difficult to justify something like this.

52
00:04:09,081 --> 00:04:12,120
It wasn't until a 1909 high-end

53
00:04:12,121 --> 00:04:16,080
bond issue that a coast route first appeared on the list of roads to be

54
00:04:16,081 --> 00:04:21,960
funded. With Southern California's quickly growing population, developers

55
00:04:21,961 --> 00:04:27,240
saw the potential in its shoreline. Construction proceeded slowly at first

56
00:04:27,241 --> 00:04:31,620
because it had to rely mostly on muzzles, not machines.

57
00:04:31,621 --> 00:04:36,120
The time that construction began on Highway 1, it was really a transition

58
00:04:36,121 --> 00:04:43,120
period between human power and horsepower, literally horsepower, and the more modern

59
00:04:43,121 --> 00:04:47,737
mechanized age. So initially, a lot of this was done by handwork.

60
00:04:47,738 --> 00:04:49,300
It was pick and shovel

61
00:04:49,301 --> 00:04:50,001
work.

62
00:04:50,002 --> 00:04:56,660
By about the mid-1920s then, we began to see the transition to more mechanized equipment.

63
00:04:56,661 --> 00:05:03,060
Steam shovels were very popular to carve and cut a roadway through this land.

64
00:05:03,061 --> 00:05:07,500
During this early work on the coast route, highway building was still a new and untested

65
00:05:07,501 --> 00:05:10,840
science of trial and error.

66
00:05:10,841 --> 00:05:15,900
Down near San Diego, a four-inch thick layer of concrete was poured as a road surface,

67
00:05:15,901 --> 00:05:20,020
but it began to break up under the weight of the traffic.

68
00:05:20,021 --> 00:05:26,240
It had to be rebuilt, only this time a grid of steel rebar was laid down first to reinforce

69
00:05:26,241 --> 00:05:28,660
the concrete.

70
00:05:28,661 --> 00:05:33,280
For a decade, Southern California's highway builders had acquired the experience and the

71
00:05:33,281 --> 00:05:38,440
technology needed to tackle a major impasse.

72
00:05:38,441 --> 00:05:43,200
Northwest of Los Angeles, a passage needed to be built between the mountains and the

73
00:05:43,201 --> 00:05:51,560
pounding surf from above Malibu to a steadfast promontory of solid rock called Point Magoo.

74
00:05:51,561 --> 00:05:56,280
The 10-mile section where the hard rock cliffs slammed right into the sea had to be surveyed

75
00:05:56,281 --> 00:05:57,280
from motorboats.

76
00:05:57,281 --> 00:06:02,320
It was clearly going to be a big job and demanded the biggest equipment available.

77
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:10,960
In order to build a highway through that stretch, the contractor had to bring in steam shovels on ocean barges,

78
00:06:11,420 --> 00:06:16,440
actually float them in on the ocean up to the coast and employ them in that area.

79
00:06:18,060 --> 00:06:23,760
In that 10 miles, massive amounts of dirt and solid rock were dug out of the sides of the mountains

80
00:06:23,761 --> 00:06:26,580
to create a shelf or bench for the road.

81
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:33,100
Benching is a technique where they'll put in heavy equipment to simply cut a

82
00:06:33,101 --> 00:06:37,360
bench into the hillside onto which the road can be placed. Now from time to

83
00:06:37,361 --> 00:06:41,800
time they may actually have to start even higher up on the hillside to create

84
00:06:41,801 --> 00:06:45,380
a bench and then work their way down to the level where the roadway will

85
00:06:45,381 --> 00:06:49,600
eventually run. And benching was a construction technique that was

86
00:06:49,601 --> 00:06:54,700
developed by mining companies and later adapted by road construction contractors.

87
00:06:54,701 --> 00:06:56,940
When the shovels couldn't cut it,

88
00:06:57,040 --> 00:07:00,000
explosive charges of up to 80,000 pounds were used

89
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:03,000
to reshape the mountains.

90
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:05,740
The way that they did it was first,

91
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:08,140
surveyors would repel down the cliffs

92
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:11,080
and figure out the depth that they needed to go.

93
00:07:11,180 --> 00:07:14,660
And then the crews would drill holes in the rock of the cliffs

94
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:17,960
and then they would fill them up with dynamite.

95
00:07:18,060 --> 00:07:19,500
They'd blow up the cliff.

96
00:07:24,700 --> 00:07:29,080
And for much of this stretch, they actually built a temporary railroad to haul the debris out.

97
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:33,620
It took five years to get this relatively short section passable.

98
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:40,020
When Route 1 rounds Point Magoo, it soon merges with Highway 101.

99
00:07:41,860 --> 00:07:46,600
It was while working on the 101, about 100 miles north of Los Angeles,

100
00:07:47,320 --> 00:07:51,180
that California's Department of Highways added bridges to their responsibilities.

101
00:07:51,181 --> 00:07:58,180
They had to find a way to get the road across Arroyo Hondo Canyon and decided an arch bridge

102
00:07:58,181 --> 00:08:00,225
offered the best solution.

103
00:08:00,226 --> 00:08:05,180
In its basic form, an arch bridge is structurally sound because

104
00:08:05,181 --> 00:08:10,180
of the way gravity compresses its elements and distributes its weight down to the ground.

105
00:08:10,181 --> 00:08:16,180
Theoretically you can build an arch and it won't need any reinforcing steel, but the designers

106
00:08:16,181 --> 00:08:19,180
of the day knew there was going to be some kind of lateral movement on the structure due to

107
00:08:19,181 --> 00:08:24,760
structure due to earthquake, due to wind, and as a result of that they put in

108
00:08:24,761 --> 00:08:28,880
reinforcement into the arch and that reinforcement obviously ties into the

109
00:08:28,881 --> 00:08:34,060
columns as well. The State Bridge engineers used the long-established

110
00:08:34,061 --> 00:08:38,940
method of erecting a wooden scaffolding called a false work to act as a frame and

111
00:08:38,941 --> 00:08:45,100
support during construction. After the concrete is poured and is hardened the

112
00:08:45,101 --> 00:08:49,160
false work is removed to reveal the finished structure.

113
00:08:49,161 --> 00:08:51,500
It's an open spandrel concrete arch bridge,

114
00:08:51,501 --> 00:08:54,600
which is concrete arch with the sides cut out

115
00:08:54,601 --> 00:08:57,400
so that just the structure itself is visible.

116
00:08:57,401 --> 00:09:01,380
It makes for less material, a lighter bridge.

117
00:09:01,381 --> 00:09:04,100
It's an excellent example of that early construction.

118
00:09:04,101 --> 00:09:09,360
Now, that bridge stayed in operation well until the 1970s.

119
00:09:09,361 --> 00:09:11,100
At the time it was built, of course,

120
00:09:11,101 --> 00:09:15,100
Automobiles were much smaller, narrower.

121
00:09:15,101 --> 00:09:18,300
The road deck, the portion of road that we actually travel on,

122
00:09:18,301 --> 00:09:19,200
was fairly narrow.

123
00:09:19,201 --> 00:09:21,900
It was just about 22 feet wide.

124
00:09:21,901 --> 00:09:24,100
So the bridge became obsolete.

125
00:09:24,101 --> 00:09:27,200
And I think some of its importance

126
00:09:27,201 --> 00:09:29,900
within the state highway establishment

127
00:09:29,901 --> 00:09:34,400
is suggested by the fact that when the 101 was rebuilt in that area,

128
00:09:34,401 --> 00:09:35,700
they saved that bridge.

129
00:09:35,701 --> 00:09:38,600
They bypassed it and kept it in place

130
00:09:38,601 --> 00:09:42,900
as a monument to the work of the department.

131
00:09:42,901 --> 00:09:45,260
By the time California's Highway Department finished

132
00:09:45,261 --> 00:09:49,580
the Arroyo Hondo Bridge in 1918, it had years of experience

133
00:09:49,581 --> 00:09:52,840
in building the Pacific Coast Highway.

134
00:09:52,841 --> 00:09:56,000
Those skills were about to be put to the ultimate test

135
00:09:56,001 --> 00:09:58,400
while building a road along the steepest coastline

136
00:09:58,401 --> 00:10:00,800
in the state, Big Sur.

137
00:10:03,980 --> 00:10:06,860
Some of the explosive powder used to blast the Pacific Coast

138
00:10:06,861 --> 00:10:11,980
Highway's path along Point Magoo was salvaged from hand grenades left over from World War

139
00:10:11,981 --> 00:10:16,820
I. The Pacific Coast Highway will return on Modern Marvels.

140
00:10:21,540 --> 00:10:27,240
Halfway between Los Angeles and San Francisco, the Pacific Coast Highway begins to hang right

141
00:10:27,241 --> 00:10:30,780
on the very edge of Central California's imposing coastal range.

142
00:10:30,781 --> 00:10:38,940
Building this section of road, highway crews were confronted with their biggest challenge.

143
00:10:38,941 --> 00:10:44,760
The dramatic cliffs and deep canyons around Big Sur are magnificent, but for engineers,

144
00:10:44,761 --> 00:10:46,880
they are a road-building nightmare.

145
00:10:46,881 --> 00:10:52,160
Along this stretch of road, as you drive from San Simeon north, this is an emerging coastline.

146
00:10:52,161 --> 00:10:58,300
Geologically speaking, you're driving along a very young geologic environment, and the

147
00:10:58,301 --> 00:11:00,260
The mountains are rising out of the sea.

148
00:11:01,020 --> 00:11:03,580
And if you drive along and you're thinking about that,

149
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:06,040
you're sort of watching geology in action.

150
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:09,300
The expression of that is landslides and rockfalls.

151
00:11:10,340 --> 00:11:14,220
And thinking about that, it's a very interesting place to drive along

152
00:11:14,221 --> 00:11:16,740
and certainly unique around the world.

153
00:11:17,760 --> 00:11:22,840
The new road was included in a 1919 California State Highway bond issue.

154
00:11:23,860 --> 00:11:26,740
Most of the surveying had to be done on horseback or on foot.

155
00:11:26,741 --> 00:11:35,640
In 1921, road work began at the Piedras Blancas Lighthouse, just above San Simeon, and headed north.

156
00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:42,560
By the fall of 1922, work had reached some of the most rugged and dangerous landscapes.

157
00:11:44,380 --> 00:11:48,040
Digging into the unstable slopes often triggered huge landslides.

158
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:56,300
There were times when steam shovels or caterpillars would be working, that slides would occur that would engulf.

159
00:11:56,301 --> 00:11:57,780
the machinery or equipment.

160
00:11:58,100 --> 00:12:00,320
I know there was one event that actually knocked a steam shovel

161
00:12:00,321 --> 00:12:02,180
about 500 feet down the cliff.

162
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:06,480
The gentleman was able to ride that particular event out.

163
00:12:06,880 --> 00:12:08,500
He got out of the cab and walked away,

164
00:12:08,501 --> 00:12:10,940
and he went looking for employment in flatter land.

165
00:12:12,460 --> 00:12:16,220
The slow and treacherous work was costing more than originally estimated

166
00:12:16,221 --> 00:12:19,560
and resulted in a three-year halt in construction.

167
00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:25,200
Then in 1927, with the decision to use prison labor,

168
00:12:25,201 --> 00:12:26,980
work started up again.

169
00:12:29,460 --> 00:12:33,200
The inmates' backbreaking efforts were rewarded with reduced sentences

170
00:12:33,201 --> 00:12:36,600
and or pay of 35 cents a day.

171
00:12:37,900 --> 00:12:41,840
Skilled civilians handled the heavy equipment and the huge quantities of dynamite.

172
00:12:46,320 --> 00:12:49,520
13 million cubic yards of earth were blown away

173
00:12:49,521 --> 00:12:51,660
to make an acceptable route for the road.

174
00:12:51,661 --> 00:12:59,500
The area between San Simeon and Monterey Bay is riddled with gorges and rivers.

175
00:12:59,501 --> 00:13:03,100
Engineers needed to design several bridges to traverse these.

176
00:13:03,101 --> 00:13:06,300
But that was just the beginning.

177
00:13:06,301 --> 00:13:12,420
The logistics of getting the materials on site, the unstable ground, and the unpredictable

178
00:13:12,421 --> 00:13:17,980
weather presented challenges to the builders, especially at an area known as Big Creek.

179
00:13:17,981 --> 00:13:22,860
In 1937, there was a large storm here on the coast.

180
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:24,900
You can see how windy it is today.

181
00:13:25,320 --> 00:13:27,960
Well, this is minor compared to what happened in 1937.

182
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:36,580
They had 70-mile-an-hour winds that actually blew down the wooden falsework that was built to construct the bridge.

183
00:13:37,800 --> 00:13:40,840
After the falsework was rebuilt and construction completed,

184
00:13:41,600 --> 00:13:44,740
The Big Creek Bridge was 597 feet long

185
00:13:44,741 --> 00:13:49,940
and comprised of two main arch spans of 177 1⁄2 feet long

186
00:13:49,941 --> 00:13:53,280
and two tied half arches of 81 feet.

187
00:13:54,280 --> 00:13:56,540
But perhaps the bridge's real genius

188
00:13:56,541 --> 00:14:00,560
is in the two 34 1⁄2 foot long swing spans

189
00:14:00,561 --> 00:14:03,460
that rest but are not attached to the abutments

190
00:14:03,461 --> 00:14:04,780
on either side of the canyon.

191
00:14:05,560 --> 00:14:09,880
At both ends of the bridge, you'll see these half arches,

192
00:14:09,881 --> 00:14:12,720
which are cantilevered off of the towers.

193
00:14:13,240 --> 00:14:17,320
This enables the deck to float free of the abutments.

194
00:14:18,300 --> 00:14:22,060
The surrounding hillsides were deemed to be so unstable

195
00:14:22,061 --> 00:14:26,100
that it was necessary to not tie the road deck

196
00:14:26,101 --> 00:14:27,680
or the bridge deck into the abutments,

197
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:29,580
allowing the bridge to float free,

198
00:14:30,020 --> 00:14:32,340
so that if there was landslide or movement

199
00:14:32,341 --> 00:14:33,580
at either end of the bridge,

200
00:14:33,940 --> 00:14:36,520
the bridge would not be disturbed or torn away

201
00:14:36,521 --> 00:14:38,080
by that landslide.

202
00:14:38,081 --> 00:14:41,280
Toward the northern end of this section of highway

203
00:14:41,281 --> 00:14:46,240
stands one of the most aesthetically spectacular and photographed spans in the world,

204
00:14:47,620 --> 00:14:48,600
the Bixby Bridge.

205
00:14:49,140 --> 00:14:54,260
Bixby Bridge is known around the world for its beautiful design.

206
00:14:54,660 --> 00:14:57,740
It's an interesting bridge because, of course, it's on a curve.

207
00:14:58,300 --> 00:15:03,080
And originally, initially, when they were looking at how to cross that enormous canyon,

208
00:15:03,540 --> 00:15:05,400
they looked at a variety of options.

209
00:15:05,401 --> 00:15:10,960
One would have been a tunnel through the mountain and a smaller bridge farther up the canyon,

210
00:15:11,200 --> 00:15:15,320
but they picked that location simply because it is spectacular.

211
00:15:16,500 --> 00:15:24,000
The 150-foot-high towers were built first, followed by the abutments and the approach slabs to the towers.

212
00:15:24,660 --> 00:15:27,800
Then the falsework was erected, and the arches were cast.

213
00:15:29,460 --> 00:15:34,400
The Bixby's designers decided that an arch design would complement the contours of the canyon.

214
00:15:35,400 --> 00:15:38,460
and that concrete would more pleasingly blend with the scenery.

215
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:43,420
From a practical standpoint, concrete was also less expensive

216
00:15:43,421 --> 00:15:46,660
and more resistant to the coastal conditions than steel.

217
00:15:47,740 --> 00:15:52,640
At 320 feet, the central concrete arch span was the longest in California.

218
00:15:53,640 --> 00:15:56,780
But the Bixby Bridge was designed with such precision

219
00:15:56,781 --> 00:16:00,020
that even though its long and slender arch might seem delicate,

220
00:16:00,860 --> 00:16:03,060
the way it distributes the load of its own weight

221
00:16:03,061 --> 00:16:07,660
and that of the traffic traveling across it gives it great strength.

222
00:16:08,260 --> 00:16:13,920
The vertical load path of the bridge will go from the T-beam deck into each of the columns

223
00:16:13,921 --> 00:16:16,140
and then from the columns into the arch.

224
00:16:16,480 --> 00:16:21,480
The arch will then take that load and distribute it to the two towers on either side of the bridge.

225
00:16:21,960 --> 00:16:23,140
That's the beauty of an arch.

226
00:16:23,141 --> 00:16:28,980
You can actually have supports for the structure that are located high up off the creek

227
00:16:28,981 --> 00:16:33,420
in case of a tsunami event or some kind of wave action

228
00:16:33,421 --> 00:16:36,760
that forces water to cause erosion of the structure.

229
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,580
The Bixby Bridge was completed in 1932

230
00:16:40,581 --> 00:16:44,740
at a cost of just over $200,000.

231
00:16:45,340 --> 00:16:48,080
This beautiful blending of technology and nature

232
00:16:48,081 --> 00:16:51,340
helped the San Simeon to Carmel portion of Route 1

233
00:16:51,341 --> 00:16:54,540
to be proclaimed California's first scenic highway.

234
00:16:54,541 --> 00:17:01,540
It's a distinction that must sound deceptively serene to those that toil to keep this very problematic road open.

235
00:17:01,541 --> 00:17:14,540
This section of road along here, along Big Sur, has been mapped with over a thousand landslides of various ages and in various stages of instability.

236
00:17:14,541 --> 00:17:23,540
So our road crosses all of these slides, or goes underneath many of them, or rides above many of these slides.

237
00:17:23,541 --> 00:17:29,000
these slides. The crumbling cliffs have blocked the road several times over the

238
00:17:29,001 --> 00:17:34,100
years, but earlier solutions such as cutting back the slopes or building

239
00:17:34,101 --> 00:17:38,940
massive retaining walls have now become unacceptable in a place famous for its

240
00:17:38,941 --> 00:17:44,780
natural beauty. Recently the California Department of Transportation began

241
00:17:44,781 --> 00:17:50,440
testing less intrusive flexible wire mesh netting developed in Europe. We put the

242
00:17:50,441 --> 00:17:56,284
netting up, we rolled rocks into it to see how much energy the nets could absorb.

243
00:17:56,285 --> 00:17:56,985
For

244
00:17:56,986 --> 00:18:03,880
example, if we rolled down a 2,000 pound rock one ton traveling at 40 or 50 miles an hour,

245
00:18:03,881 --> 00:18:08,520
these barriers were protecting it without any damage to the barrier, hitting the net

246
00:18:08,521 --> 00:18:12,540
with 70, 80, 90 foot tons of impact energy.

247
00:18:12,541 --> 00:18:16,560
That kind of energy, if it hit a car, would

248
00:18:16,561 --> 00:18:18,560
push the car off the cliff.

249
00:18:18,561 --> 00:18:22,560
The netting has been installed in various places along the highway

250
00:18:22,561 --> 00:18:30,720
and has also been used to neutralize an entire cliff called rain rocks for doing just that onto the highway below.

251
00:18:31,620 --> 00:18:36,360
And the way the contractors did it, they came in, put anchors along the brow,

252
00:18:36,920 --> 00:18:40,940
and then with helicopters, flew in the nets and laid them on the slope.

253
00:18:40,941 --> 00:18:48,580
And then people would repel down and fasten things up so that it knitted all together, if you will.

254
00:18:49,920 --> 00:18:57,780
Covering the cliff with this netting has helped keep this world-famous stretch of highway open and safe for all those who come to enjoy its scenic wonders.

255
00:18:59,580 --> 00:19:08,160
But just up the road, even this breakthrough technology isn't enough to tame a disaster-prone spot called the Devil's Slide.

256
00:19:10,940 --> 00:19:17,040
In the year 2000, a seismic retrofit was completed on the Bixby Bridge to make it more earthquake-safe.

257
00:19:17,740 --> 00:19:25,920
The retrofit's cost of $5.2 million was 26 times greater than the bridge's original construction cost in 1932.

258
00:19:26,560 --> 00:19:29,880
Pacific Coast Highway will return on Modern Marvels.

259
00:19:33,780 --> 00:19:39,180
After the wild ride through the twists and turns along Central California's coastal mountain range,

260
00:19:39,181 --> 00:19:43,980
Route 1 circumvents the tranquil beauty of Monterey Bay.

261
00:19:46,060 --> 00:19:53,780
But it's only a brief respite before the road is again wedged precariously between the sea and unstable mountains.

262
00:19:56,360 --> 00:20:03,700
Just south of the outskirts of San Francisco is one of the most troublesome spots along the entire Pacific Coast Highway system.

263
00:20:04,480 --> 00:20:07,460
It is aptly named the Devil's Slide.

264
00:20:09,180 --> 00:20:16,180
Before there was a road, the ill-fated Oceanshore Railroad traversed it.

265
00:20:16,181 --> 00:20:23,340
They started in 1905, and of course in April of 1906, the great earthquake threw a wrench

266
00:20:23,341 --> 00:20:28,000
into that plan, and it actually did pitch some of the rails off the mountain and into

267
00:20:28,001 --> 00:20:29,000
the ocean.

268
00:20:29,001 --> 00:20:35,220
They began to rebuild shortly thereafter, but the very loose and deteriorated nature

269
00:20:35,221 --> 00:20:37,060
of the rock in that area.

270
00:20:37,061 --> 00:20:40,600
It's constantly sliding and shifting.

271
00:20:40,601 --> 00:20:43,720
Several landslides later, the Devil's Slide

272
00:20:43,721 --> 00:20:47,700
drove the railroad into bankruptcy.

273
00:20:47,701 --> 00:20:51,580
In 1914, a local road replaced the rails.

274
00:20:51,581 --> 00:20:54,300
And in 1933, the State Highway Department

275
00:20:54,301 --> 00:20:59,740
took over the road and made it part of the coast route.

276
00:20:59,741 --> 00:21:03,120
Landslides have damaged the road many times.

277
00:21:03,121 --> 00:21:06,360
and highway crews keep patching it back together.

278
00:21:06,361 --> 00:21:11,140
They've tried adding complex drainage systems and netting,

279
00:21:11,141 --> 00:21:14,400
but now it's clear that the only real lasting solution

280
00:21:14,401 --> 00:21:16,940
would be to reroute the highway inland

281
00:21:16,941 --> 00:21:20,100
and avoid the Devil's Slide altogether.

282
00:21:20,101 --> 00:21:23,060
In a way, just as Devil's Slide represents

283
00:21:23,061 --> 00:21:25,880
one of the great failures of the Coast Highway,

284
00:21:25,881 --> 00:21:27,900
not far north of there is certainly

285
00:21:27,901 --> 00:21:29,500
one of the great achievements and one

286
00:21:29,501 --> 00:21:32,720
of the most spectacular spots on the road to this day,

287
00:21:32,721 --> 00:21:36,060
where the 1 and the 101 meet just at the north end of San Francisco

288
00:21:36,061 --> 00:21:38,800
and go across the Golden Gate Bridge,

289
00:21:39,700 --> 00:21:43,000
one of the most recognizable landmarks anywhere.

290
00:21:44,340 --> 00:21:51,140
The Golden Gate Bridge was constructed between January 1933 and November of 1936.

291
00:21:51,680 --> 00:21:58,140
It was a monumental undertaking that cost $33 million and the lives of 11 workers.

292
00:21:58,141 --> 00:22:04,960
at a total length of 8,981 feet, it is one of the longest single-span bridges ever built.

293
00:22:05,760 --> 00:22:12,480
It's a suspension bridge in design, which means that the central span is held in position by the vertical cables

294
00:22:12,481 --> 00:22:16,180
that are attached to the two main cables slung high across the bridge.

295
00:22:17,220 --> 00:22:23,540
They transmit the weight of the deck and the live load to the towers and anchorages at both ends of the bridge.

296
00:22:23,541 --> 00:22:32,540
The designers of the Golden Gate Bridge managed to infuse the structure with such a sense of elegance that it almost masks how massive it really is.

297
00:22:32,541 --> 00:22:39,540
The two 756-foot-high towers were the tallest in the world when constructed.

298
00:22:39,541 --> 00:22:44,540
The diameters of the main cables are just over three feet.

299
00:22:44,541 --> 00:22:49,540
The weight of all the cables and their accessories is in excess of 24,000 tons.

300
00:22:49,541 --> 00:22:52,540
In the Golden Gate Bridge, it was clear the builders knew

301
00:22:52,541 --> 00:22:54,540
they were doing something special.

302
00:22:54,541 --> 00:22:58,540
You look at the thing today, and unlike the gray or silver,

303
00:22:58,541 --> 00:23:02,540
you know, blending colors of virtually any other bridge you

304
00:23:02,541 --> 00:23:06,540
can think of, the Golden Gate is this bright red-orange.

305
00:23:06,541 --> 00:23:07,540
It stands out.

306
00:23:07,541 --> 00:23:08,540
It calls attention to itself.

307
00:23:08,541 --> 00:23:10,540
It says, hey, I'm here.

308
00:23:10,541 --> 00:23:13,540
We did this.

309
00:23:13,541 --> 00:23:16,540
On the grade at the northern end of the Golden Gate Bridge

310
00:23:16,541 --> 00:23:18,540
is the Waldo Tunnel.

311
00:23:18,541 --> 00:23:23,080
The original one-bore tunnel was completed in 1937.

312
00:23:24,600 --> 00:23:29,420
By the 1950s, the increase in traffic made it necessary to widen the highway.

313
00:23:30,380 --> 00:23:35,140
But to widen the tunnel would have required the closure of this vital artery into San Francisco.

314
00:23:36,040 --> 00:23:41,520
The solution was to drill a second bore through the mountain to accommodate the two new lanes of highway.

315
00:23:41,521 --> 00:23:50,220
Not too far beyond the Waldo Tunnel, the coast route heads back to the shoreline and reverts

316
00:23:50,221 --> 00:23:54,380
to a two-lane, very windy road.

317
00:23:54,381 --> 00:23:59,240
Northern California's constantly shifting ground results in bulges and sags in the roadway

318
00:23:59,241 --> 00:24:05,400
and is the reason why asphalt, instead of concrete, is the most practical road service

319
00:24:05,401 --> 00:24:06,900
under these conditions.

320
00:24:06,901 --> 00:24:13,900
Asphalt is used on a lot of roadways and one of the main reasons it works in California

321
00:24:13,901 --> 00:24:16,900
and particularly on Highway 1 is the fact that it's flexible.

322
00:24:16,901 --> 00:24:20,900
The road base itself is flexible and the asphalt on top is also flexible

323
00:24:20,901 --> 00:24:24,900
in that when the land underneath moves, which it does a lot out here,

324
00:24:24,901 --> 00:24:29,900
in particular on the coast, the road moves with it.

325
00:24:29,901 --> 00:24:32,900
And also repairs are very easy if you have to go out and make one.

326
00:24:32,901 --> 00:24:37,540
whereas with concrete, once it breaks, it's very difficult to go in and repair it.

327
00:24:37,660 --> 00:24:40,860
It does not flex in the same way that asphalt does.

328
00:24:41,880 --> 00:24:47,420
About 200 miles north of San Francisco, Highway 1 cuts inland for the last time,

329
00:24:47,940 --> 00:24:50,880
and Highway 101 becomes the coast route from here on.

330
00:24:52,080 --> 00:24:56,220
The decision was made that it would be just too hard, too expensive,

331
00:24:56,221 --> 00:24:59,880
and would serve too few travelers to warrant continuing Route 1

332
00:24:59,881 --> 00:25:02,740
all the way up northern California's mountainous coast.

333
00:25:02,900 --> 00:25:07,140
Road builders carved the path of the 101 through majestic

334
00:25:07,200 --> 00:25:11,140
redwood groves found along the banks of the Eel River.

335
00:25:11,200 --> 00:25:15,040
But keeping the road off the coast didn't mean it was safe

336
00:25:15,120 --> 00:25:19,520
from disaster. In 1964, tropical rains and a quickly

337
00:25:19,580 --> 00:25:25,260
melting snowpack turned the Eel River into a roaring torrent.

338
00:25:25,320 --> 00:25:28,260
They called it the Killer Eel in 64.

339
00:25:28,261 --> 00:25:33,800
There was just logs and houses and debris coming down the river,

340
00:25:33,920 --> 00:25:39,600
and that lodged in some of the bridges in the piers and in the truss work of the bridges,

341
00:25:39,780 --> 00:25:42,720
and that's why some of the bridges failed.

342
00:25:43,460 --> 00:25:46,860
Over 150 miles of this section of the highway were flooded,

343
00:25:47,820 --> 00:25:50,900
leaving damaged road and debris that would take months to clean up.

344
00:25:53,720 --> 00:25:56,900
Plans had already been in the works to widen the road in this area,

345
00:25:56,901 --> 00:26:05,980
But instead of using the old route right along the river, engineers rebuilt the road above the high water mark of the 1964 flood.

346
00:26:07,120 --> 00:26:17,833
Now travelers were assured a safer passage up to Oregon, where the dramatic coastline and wetter environment confronted road and bridge builders with a new set of

347
00:26:17,834 --> 00:26:18,560
challenges.

348
00:26:20,640 --> 00:26:26,000
A 1995 landslide closed the coast highway at the Devil's Slide for five months.

349
00:26:26,900 --> 00:26:30,760
it cost nearly one and a half million dollars to reopen the road.

350
00:26:31,660 --> 00:26:34,980
The Pacific Coast Highway will return on Modern Marvels.

351
00:26:37,920 --> 00:26:41,980
When the U.S. 101 crosses the border from California into Oregon,

352
00:26:42,600 --> 00:26:46,800
it becomes what is now referred to as the Pacific Coast Scenic Byway.

353
00:26:47,540 --> 00:26:53,320
The nearly 400-mile route along Oregon's sometimes jagged and often rain-soaked shore

354
00:26:53,321 --> 00:26:56,820
began with just two miles of pavement in 1914

355
00:26:56,821 --> 00:27:00,120
and took over 18 years to complete.

356
00:27:02,840 --> 00:27:05,720
A major bridge-building program was also funded.

357
00:27:07,280 --> 00:27:09,880
Many of the most dramatic and artful spans

358
00:27:09,881 --> 00:27:12,420
were designed by Oregon's master bridge-builder,

359
00:27:12,960 --> 00:27:15,340
a man named Condi B. McCullough.

360
00:27:16,600 --> 00:27:19,240
C.B. McCullough came to Oregon in 1916

361
00:27:19,241 --> 00:27:23,220
as a professor at what is today Oregon State University.

362
00:27:23,320 --> 00:27:24,487
in Corvallis.

363
00:27:24,488 --> 00:27:30,860
As luck would have it, Oregon needed to build highways as part of that,

364
00:27:30,940 --> 00:27:33,024
bridges, right after World War I.

365
00:27:33,025 --> 00:27:36,560
And McCullough became the state bridge engineer in 1919.

366
00:27:37,880 --> 00:27:42,020
McCullough believed that bridges could be built both economically and aesthetically.

367
00:27:43,160 --> 00:27:47,518
Fourteen of the bridges along the Coast Highway are his designs.

368
00:27:47,519 --> 00:27:49,220
They range from small and

369
00:27:49,221 --> 00:27:54,460
practical to impressive structures of steel and concrete that leap for

370
00:27:54,461 --> 00:27:59,000
thousands of feet across Oregon's bays. He really had a blank canvas here in

371
00:27:59,001 --> 00:28:04,640
Oregon for bridges and he was able to tap all of his talents in creating a

372
00:28:04,641 --> 00:28:09,340
wonderful collection for the state of Oregon. The Rogue River Bridge built in

373
00:28:09,341 --> 00:28:17,000
1931 is made up of seven 230-foot arch spans and 18 concrete approach spans.

374
00:28:17,001 --> 00:28:21,000
Its total length is nearly 1,900 feet.

375
00:28:21,001 --> 00:28:24,000
McCollum made it the first structure in the United States

376
00:28:24,001 --> 00:28:27,000
to use an innovative pre-compression technique

377
00:28:27,001 --> 00:28:30,000
developed by French engineer Eugène Frassinet

378
00:28:30,001 --> 00:28:34,000
that compensates for natural settling that occurs in arch bridges.

379
00:28:34,001 --> 00:28:39,000
Normally when an arch bridge is constructed, it's like that.

380
00:28:39,001 --> 00:28:42,000
And when you take the false work underneath it,

381
00:28:42,001 --> 00:28:45,000
it tends to just lower itself a little bit.

382
00:28:45,001 --> 00:28:49,420
little bit. On this bridge, McCullough designed the pieces in two separate

383
00:28:49,421 --> 00:28:56,760
parts, used six 100-ton hydraulic jacks to jack it apart, put a closure pour in

384
00:28:56,761 --> 00:29:03,240
there so that the bridge was preloaded, and then he removed the faults work. So as

385
00:29:03,241 --> 00:29:10,620
a result, here we are nearly 80 years later, and the bridge is just nice and

386
00:29:10,621 --> 00:29:16,360
level. The Fresenay method also enabled McCullough to use less steel and concrete,

387
00:29:16,361 --> 00:29:21,840
which allowed him to incorporate thinner ribs that add to the aesthetic appeal of the bridge.

388
00:29:23,980 --> 00:29:32,180
Further up the coast, work began on the Coos Bay Bridge in 1934. At 5,305 feet,

389
00:29:32,740 --> 00:29:35,900
it's the longest and most expensive of the McCullough-designed bridges.

390
00:29:35,901 --> 00:29:42,900
He chose a steel cantilever truss because the volume of shipping made a drawbridge impractical.

391
00:29:42,901 --> 00:29:49,900
The cantilever truss enabled McCullough to build a high and wide shipping channel.

392
00:29:49,901 --> 00:29:55,900
It consists of anchor arms spanning from the abutments to the main piers,

393
00:29:55,901 --> 00:29:59,900
cantilever arms that form part of the main span,

394
00:29:59,901 --> 00:30:06,900
and a suspended span, which is hung from the ends of the two cantilever arms to bridge the gap between them.

395
00:30:06,901 --> 00:30:12,900
Each of the anchor arms balances the weight of the corresponding cantilever arm.

396
00:30:12,901 --> 00:30:19,900
The two main towers that support the truss rise 280 feet above the water.

397
00:30:19,901 --> 00:30:26,900
The flanking spans of it are very large concrete arch structures.

398
00:30:26,901 --> 00:30:31,500
They are decorated in McCullough-style arctico,

399
00:30:31,920 --> 00:30:36,280
but you don't really appreciate Gothic arches in a structure

400
00:30:36,281 --> 00:30:41,220
until you drive onto the bridge and you go through the truss

401
00:30:41,221 --> 00:30:46,800
because the cross members form Gothic arches in themselves

402
00:30:46,801 --> 00:30:50,600
and you have the feeling that you're driving through a cathedral.

403
00:30:50,601 --> 00:30:58,700
At Cape Creek, McCullough paid his respects to the ancient bridge builders of Europe with

404
00:30:58,701 --> 00:31:00,580
a Roman aqueduct-inspired design.

405
00:31:00,581 --> 00:31:06,454
Well, here again, McCullough is finding the best bridge for the location.

406
00:31:06,455 --> 00:31:07,420
He has sandy

407
00:31:07,421 --> 00:31:13,080
soil foundations there that cannot really handle the heavy load of a bridge at particular

408
00:31:13,081 --> 00:31:17,477
locations. His goal here is to disperse the bridge's load. So how do you do that?

409
00:31:17,478 --> 00:31:18,178
You

410
00:31:18,179 --> 00:31:20,040
put it on many, many feet or many legs.

411
00:31:20,041 --> 00:31:23,400
And he achieved that through his use of a double-deck viaduct.

412
00:31:23,401 --> 00:31:28,200
And then he threw his little arch over Cape Creek.

413
00:31:28,201 --> 00:31:32,880
In the city of Newport stands the 3,223-foot-long

414
00:31:32,881 --> 00:31:36,200
Yaquina Bay Bridge.

415
00:31:36,201 --> 00:31:40,040
Built in 1936, McCullough avoided building one bridge

416
00:31:40,041 --> 00:31:44,000
on a gigantic scale by designing what is, in essence,

417
00:31:44,001 --> 00:31:47,120
a series of small, sturdy bridges that are linked together

418
00:31:47,121 --> 00:31:47,900
across the bay.

419
00:31:49,840 --> 00:31:55,280
It's a wonderful structure with a series of reinforced concrete deck arches, a 600-foot

420
00:31:55,281 --> 00:32:00,960
steel through arch, another steel deck arch, and a few more reinforced concrete deck arches.

421
00:32:02,160 --> 00:32:08,700
This bridge has very spectacular detailing on it once again.

422
00:32:08,701 --> 00:32:22,100
Very, very much of McCullough's work picks up on artistic values of Europe and applied current technology, cutting-edge technology of that time.

423
00:32:23,980 --> 00:32:32,200
By the time Oregon completed its coastal highway bridges in 1936, Washington had finished its portion of the 101 as well.

424
00:32:32,201 --> 00:32:39,900
But for another 29 years, there remained a four-mile-wide gap in the road between Oregon and Washington,

425
00:32:40,460 --> 00:32:42,460
the mouth of the Columbia River.

426
00:32:43,740 --> 00:32:47,700
As traffic increased from the 1930s through the 1950s,

427
00:32:47,900 --> 00:32:53,600
the number of ferries used to get vehicles across the Columbia rose from one to four.

428
00:32:54,340 --> 00:32:56,980
The four ferries was about all the docks could handle.

429
00:32:56,981 --> 00:33:04,620
In the summertime, they ran just as fast as they could back and forth from early in the morning until 10 o'clock at night,

430
00:33:04,780 --> 00:33:07,080
but the traffic was just more than they could handle.

431
00:33:07,860 --> 00:33:11,760
In 1962, construction of a bridge finally began.

432
00:33:12,560 --> 00:33:15,800
Critics who fought funding the project had called it a bridge to nowhere,

433
00:33:16,700 --> 00:33:18,840
a name that didn't sit well with the people of Washington.

434
00:33:18,841 --> 00:33:24,280
The need for a tall and wide main shipping channel

435
00:33:24,281 --> 00:33:27,180
made a steel truss the most practical choice,

436
00:33:27,680 --> 00:33:29,480
just as it had been at Coos Bay.

437
00:33:30,560 --> 00:33:33,540
Here again, the weight of the long central suspension span

438
00:33:33,541 --> 00:33:36,260
in the middle of the bridge is counterbalanced

439
00:33:36,261 --> 00:33:39,400
by the flanking spans on the other sides of the main piers.

440
00:33:40,860 --> 00:33:46,720
The main shipping span is 1,232 feet,

441
00:33:46,721 --> 00:33:49,900
and it's 200 feet above the water,

442
00:33:50,460 --> 00:33:52,180
which was high enough that would have allowed

443
00:33:52,181 --> 00:33:54,860
the largest naval vessel to have passed

444
00:33:54,861 --> 00:33:56,180
under the structure at high tide.

445
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:01,020
After passing through the steel truss

446
00:34:01,021 --> 00:34:02,340
on the Oregon side of the bridge,

447
00:34:02,980 --> 00:34:05,200
the next 10,000 feet of roadway

448
00:34:05,201 --> 00:34:07,620
runs at just 25 feet above sea level.

449
00:34:09,420 --> 00:34:12,460
Then it climbs again to 49 feet of clearance

450
00:34:12,461 --> 00:34:13,780
for the northern shipping channel.

451
00:34:13,781 --> 00:34:20,560
pre-stressed concrete beam spans set on sturdy concrete piers were made part of the design.

452
00:34:22,700 --> 00:34:27,960
They ensured that the bridge would be able to withstand not only 150 mile an hour winds from

453
00:34:27,961 --> 00:34:35,380
Pacific storms, but also the floodwaters of the Columbia River. When completed,

454
00:34:36,420 --> 00:34:41,500
the Astoria Bridge's 4.1 mile span connecting Astoria, Oregon to Megler, Washington,

455
00:34:41,501 --> 00:34:44,780
made it the longest bridge on the Pacific Coast Highway.

456
00:34:46,940 --> 00:34:49,400
Those who had dismissed it as the bridge to nowhere

457
00:34:49,401 --> 00:34:51,440
were quickly proven wrong.

458
00:34:52,720 --> 00:34:55,520
Within five months of its August 1966 opening,

459
00:34:56,280 --> 00:34:58,080
close to a quarter of a million vehicles

460
00:34:58,081 --> 00:34:59,060
had crossed the bridge.

461
00:35:01,040 --> 00:35:04,460
An uninterrupted drive along all of America's west coast

462
00:35:04,461 --> 00:35:06,160
was finally complete.

463
00:35:08,160 --> 00:35:10,060
But the work was far from over.

464
00:35:10,061 --> 00:35:20,017
The dramatic and varied topography along the route of the Pacific Coast Highway that makes the road so special is also the very reason why it is one of the most

465
00:35:20,018 --> 00:35:23,440
difficult and expensive roads in the world to maintain.

466
00:35:26,280 --> 00:35:35,320
When the last of McCullough's Bay Bridges was completed in 1936, tourism on Oregon's Coast Highway jumped 72% the following year.

467
00:35:35,740 --> 00:35:39,660
The Pacific Coast Highway will return on Modern Marvels.

468
00:35:40,060 --> 00:35:47,060
The Pacific Coast Highway is a road of endless maintenance challenges.

469
00:35:47,061 --> 00:35:53,060
Where it's sandwiched between the mountains and the sea in California,

470
00:35:53,061 --> 00:35:58,060
there's a never-ending battle with the natural forces of erosion.

471
00:35:58,061 --> 00:36:03,060
During the dry summer and early fall months, highway crews can often be found using

472
00:36:03,061 --> 00:36:08,060
earth-moving equipment to haul away debris and loose rock as a preventative measure.

473
00:36:08,061 --> 00:36:12,340
But when the winter storms return, it's one emergency after another.

474
00:36:13,300 --> 00:36:19,100
California has been referred to as kind of a theme park of disasters for this country.

475
00:36:20,380 --> 00:36:23,260
And Pacific Coast Highway is the roller coaster.

476
00:36:27,380 --> 00:36:33,800
When storms hit Southern California, floodwaters often rush out of the canyons and up and over portions of the road.

477
00:36:33,801 --> 00:36:42,340
In 1938, at Santa Monica Canyon, there was a major flood that came down, and Pacific

478
00:36:42,341 --> 00:36:47,660
Coast Highway was this 300-foot-long waterfall going into the ocean.

479
00:36:48,180 --> 00:36:55,240
But also, after it's rained, and this is sort of the joke that nature plays on us, we're

480
00:36:55,241 --> 00:37:00,420
all starting to dry out, and about a month to two months later is when all the landslides

481
00:37:00,421 --> 00:37:01,121
happen.

482
00:37:01,122 --> 00:37:04,800
Landslides can occur when the winter rains oversaturate the

483
00:37:04,840 --> 00:37:09,800
permeable cliffs, adding weight and lessening the cohesion of the soil.

484
00:37:09,840 --> 00:37:16,800
In 1958, one of the biggest occurred and became known as the Killer Slide.

485
00:37:16,840 --> 00:37:23,800
On the morning of March 27th, a sudden collapse left a 25-foot pile of earth on PCH.

486
00:37:23,840 --> 00:37:27,800
The work to reopen the road took days.

487
00:37:27,801 --> 00:37:29,880
They brought in a huge amount of equipment,

488
00:37:30,600 --> 00:37:34,260
and near the end of the construction,

489
00:37:34,540 --> 00:37:37,840
where they thought they had the road completely cut

490
00:37:37,841 --> 00:37:38,940
and was ready to go,

491
00:37:39,340 --> 00:37:40,800
they were making a determination

492
00:37:40,801 --> 00:37:44,260
as to whether the road should be reopened

493
00:37:44,261 --> 00:37:47,740
when the bluff suddenly cut loose again.

494
00:37:49,500 --> 00:37:52,480
This time, 600,000 tons of earth

495
00:37:52,481 --> 00:37:54,480
collapsed towards the crew below.

496
00:37:54,481 --> 00:38:00,980
The men on bulldozers amazingly managed to ride up onto the slide and survive, but their

497
00:38:00,981 --> 00:38:07,000
supervisor was on foot and wasn't able to outrun it.

498
00:38:07,001 --> 00:38:12,120
To avoid further catastrophes like the killer slide, many types of retaining walls have been

499
00:38:12,121 --> 00:38:17,160
erected over the years in attempts to hold back the earth in this area.

500
00:38:17,161 --> 00:38:21,360
But now at one site, there's an elaborately designed new structure that has pushed the

501
00:38:21,361 --> 00:38:25,520
science of retaining walls to another level.

502
00:38:25,521 --> 00:38:30,400
Some of the major features of this wall, first off, are these horizontal drains here.

503
00:38:30,401 --> 00:38:36,960
These are slotted PVC pipes that go back about 250 feet into the slope, and they collect

504
00:38:36,961 --> 00:38:40,880
water along their length and bring it out to the front here where it drains away.

505
00:38:40,881 --> 00:38:44,900
As you can see, this one is working pretty well right now.

506
00:38:44,901 --> 00:38:51,176
Now this is a tie back. This is drilled about 120 feet back into the hillside.

507
00:38:51,177 --> 00:38:51,900
It's an 8

508
00:38:51,940 --> 00:38:58,940
inch diameter hole and strands are inserted into that and grouted in place 120 feet back.

509
00:38:59,340 --> 00:39:03,960
And then on this side of the wall they get tensioned, kind of like a post tension bridge

510
00:39:03,961 --> 00:39:09,300
where we have a big jack we put on this side and we pull all those strands to a specified

511
00:39:09,301 --> 00:39:13,840
force and then lock it off with this big nut.

512
00:39:13,841 --> 00:39:19,760
Each tieback is designed to withstand about three to three hundred fifty thousand pounds

513
00:39:19,761 --> 00:39:20,760
of pressure.

514
00:39:20,761 --> 00:39:26,600
So, as you can tell, we have a number of these tiebacks here which should alleviate the pressure

515
00:39:26,601 --> 00:39:30,800
that the mountain is exerting on itself.

516
00:39:30,801 --> 00:39:35,460
Aside from disaster repair and prevention, one of the biggest maintenance issues facing

517
00:39:35,461 --> 00:39:40,700
the entire highway and its bridges is simply age.

518
00:39:40,701 --> 00:39:43,904
A half a century after it was built, one of Condi B.

519
00:39:43,905 --> 00:39:46,060
McCullough's beloved Oregon bridges

520
00:39:46,061 --> 00:39:48,320
was deemed beyond repair.

521
00:39:48,321 --> 00:39:53,200
The old Elsie Bay Bridge, like many of the other bridges along the Oregon Coast Highway,

522
00:39:53,201 --> 00:39:55,360
are in a very severe climate.

523
00:39:55,361 --> 00:39:57,900
They're close to the ocean.

524
00:39:57,901 --> 00:40:00,500
This in the end caused the bridge to fail.

525
00:40:00,501 --> 00:40:06,780
And salt water got into the concrete, seeped into the concrete, attacked the reinforcing

526
00:40:06,781 --> 00:40:09,260
bar and undermined the bridge.

527
00:40:10,700 --> 00:40:16,340
In 1992, the magnificent multiple-arched Alsea Bay Bridge was blown up.

528
00:40:35,140 --> 00:40:38,740
The new Alsea Bay Bridge, with its bold, Y-shaped piers,

529
00:40:38,741 --> 00:40:41,220
is modern bridge building at its best.

530
00:40:42,460 --> 00:40:45,260
But out of deference to McCullough's historic design,

531
00:40:45,860 --> 00:40:48,380
it also features an impressive steel arch.

532
00:40:50,860 --> 00:40:54,020
Another of McCullough's beloved bridges, the Rogue River Bridge,

533
00:40:54,400 --> 00:40:55,880
was also showing its age.

534
00:40:56,660 --> 00:40:59,200
But now it's being given a new lease on life

535
00:40:59,201 --> 00:41:01,220
thanks to an innovative preservation method

536
00:41:01,221 --> 00:41:03,180
called cathodic protection.

537
00:41:04,480 --> 00:41:07,560
This scientific breakthrough uses an electric current

538
00:41:07,561 --> 00:41:10,560
to drive the negative ions that cause corrosion

539
00:41:10,561 --> 00:41:12,700
to an applied zinc coating

540
00:41:12,701 --> 00:41:15,500
instead of the steel rebar embedded in the concrete.

541
00:41:17,280 --> 00:41:19,900
But first, the aging bridge must be thoroughly checked

542
00:41:19,901 --> 00:41:22,940
for any damaged concrete or exposed rebar.

543
00:41:23,700 --> 00:41:26,320
When the rebar corrodes, it actually expands.

544
00:41:26,460 --> 00:41:29,180
It can get up to seven times its original diameter,

545
00:41:29,400 --> 00:41:31,760
and that causes the concrete to spall.

546
00:41:31,761 --> 00:41:33,920
So it's the rebar corrosion that causes

547
00:41:33,921 --> 00:41:35,340
most of the failure in the bridges.

548
00:41:35,341 --> 00:41:38,340
We have three preliminary surveys.

549
00:41:38,341 --> 00:41:46,740
Two of them are to locate metal and then rebar that's within a half inch of the surface because

550
00:41:46,741 --> 00:41:50,800
it will short out the zinc coating that we're putting on and bring it in contact with the

551
00:41:50,801 --> 00:41:51,800
rebar.

552
00:41:51,801 --> 00:41:58,460
And then we do a D-LAM survey, which is where a guy goes over the surface and taps on every

553
00:41:58,461 --> 00:42:05,300
square inch with a hammer and listens in a hollow sound means the concrete is bad.

554
00:42:05,301 --> 00:42:06,740
and we remove it and replace it.

555
00:42:06,741 --> 00:42:11,100
And a ringing sound means the concrete is good.

556
00:42:11,101 --> 00:42:13,080
Once any faulty concrete and rebar

557
00:42:13,081 --> 00:42:16,900
have been repaired or replaced, workers wearing special suits

558
00:42:16,901 --> 00:42:19,640
to protect their lungs from harmful overspray

559
00:42:19,641 --> 00:42:22,980
and their eyes from the blinding light apply the zinc coating.

560
00:42:25,720 --> 00:42:29,260
The zinc comes in a wire form on spools,

561
00:42:29,261 --> 00:42:32,840
and we feed it through a zinc machine, which actually brings

562
00:42:32,841 --> 00:42:34,560
the zinc up to the melting point.

563
00:42:34,561 --> 00:42:37,860
and we spray molten zinc onto the surface

564
00:42:37,861 --> 00:42:41,860
and build up a 15 to 17 mil coating.

565
00:42:41,861 --> 00:42:44,680
The idea is we apply a uniform zinc coating

566
00:42:44,681 --> 00:42:46,980
over the entire surface of the bridge,

567
00:42:46,981 --> 00:42:49,480
and then we make some electrical connections

568
00:42:49,481 --> 00:42:51,600
between that coating and the rebar

569
00:42:51,601 --> 00:42:54,840
and apply a low voltage current to it

570
00:42:54,841 --> 00:42:56,380
once the project's completed.

571
00:42:57,820 --> 00:43:00,580
The zinc coating sacrifices itself

572
00:43:00,581 --> 00:43:03,520
and will protect the bridge in estimated 15 to 20 years.

573
00:43:04,740 --> 00:43:09,160
Then successive coatings can be applied to extend the life of the bridge well into the future.

574
00:43:11,620 --> 00:43:13,780
Along with their efforts in bridge preservation,

575
00:43:14,280 --> 00:43:18,980
a recently built structure at Brush Creek has put the Oregon Department of Transportation

576
00:43:18,981 --> 00:43:22,060
at the forefront of new bridge engineering as well.

577
00:43:23,200 --> 00:43:29,300
We did two innovative things on this to help expand the life of the bridge radically.

578
00:43:29,301 --> 00:43:35,880
So we went to microsilica concrete, which is a very dense, low permeability concrete.

579
00:43:36,100 --> 00:43:39,540
That means that salt water takes forever to go through it.

580
00:43:40,080 --> 00:43:46,340
The other thing that we did was we used stainless steel reinforcing in the deck and the crossbeam.

581
00:43:48,280 --> 00:43:57,160
These innovations have made the Brush Creek Bridge the first bridge designed to survive at least 120 years in the punishing coastal environment.

582
00:43:59,300 --> 00:44:08,160
After almost 100 years of floods, landslides, earthquakes, and the relentlessly pounding

583
00:44:08,161 --> 00:44:14,060
surf, the Pacific Coast Highway tenaciously clings to America's West Coast.

584
00:44:14,061 --> 00:44:18,580
With the recent technological breakthroughs, and the continuing vigilance and hard work

585
00:44:18,581 --> 00:44:24,740
by the state highway departments of California, Oregon, and Washington, motorists can still

586
00:44:24,741 --> 00:44:30,500
experience an unparalleled journey through natural beauty, along a great human achievement.

